A `UserService` is a **service-layer class** that encapsulates business logic for user-related operations. It sits between your API controllers/routes and your data access layer (repository/database), keeping concerns separated and your code testable.

### 🔹 Core Responsibilities
- Validate & transform input data
- Enforce business rules (e.g., unique emails, password strength)
- Handle sensitive operations (password hashing, token generation)
- Orchestrate multi-step workflows (registration, password reset, account deactivation)
- Never expose raw entities or secrets to the controller layer

### 🔹 Typical Methods
```ts
createUser(data)
getUserById(id)
findUserByEmail(email)
updateUser(id, data)
deleteUser(id)
hashPassword(password)
validatePassword(input, hash)
```

### 🔹 Where It Fits in Architecture
```
Controller / Route
    ↓
UserService (business logic)
    ↓
UserRepository (data access)
    ↓
Database
```
Injected via dependency injection for testability and loose coupling.

---

### 📦 Example (TypeScript / Node.js, framework-agnostic)
```ts
import { UserRepository } from './user.repository';
import { hash, compare } from 'bcrypt';

export class UserService {
  constructor(private repo: UserRepository) {}

  async createUser(data: { email: string; password: string; name: string }) {
    const existing = await this.repo.findByEmail(data.email);
    if (existing) throw new Error('Email already registered');

    const hashed = await hash(data.password, 12);
    const user = await this.repo.create({ ...data, password: hashed });

    // Never return the password
    return { id: user.id, email: user.email, name: user.name };
  }

  async getUserById(id: string) {
    const user = await this.repo.findById(id);
    if (!user) throw new Error('User not found');
    return user;
  }

  async verifyPassword(input: string, hash: string) {
    return compare(input, hash);
  }
}
```

### ✅ Best Practices
| Do | Don't |
|---|---|
| Use DTOs & validate input | Pass raw request bodies to the repo |
| Keep methods focused (SRP) | Bundle auth, billing, and user logic together |
| Return plain objects or safe DTOs | Leak passwords, tokens, or internal fields |
| Use dependency injection | Hardcode DB connections or services |
| Throw domain-specific errors | Use generic `throw new Error(...)` everywhere |

